    "lawCode": "Amd §118, Civ Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S838A\n\nSPONSOR: MONTGOMERY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the civil service law, in relation to\nimplementing a state policy of setting salaries on the basis of equiv-\nalent value of work\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would implement a state policy to ensure public employees\nreceive fair and equal pay for substantially similar work.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends paragraph (c) of subdivision 1 of section\n118 of the civil service law to ensure the principle of fair and equal\npay for substantially similar work in terms of skill, effort, responsi-\nbility and working conditions for employees with status in a protected\nclass.\n\nSection two of the bill adds a new paragraph (c) to section 118 of the\ncivil service law to provide that employees with status in a protected\nclass shall not be paid a wage less than employees without status within\na protected or with status in the same or different protected class.\n\nSection three of the bill sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhile newspapers carry banner headlines about the advances women have\nmade in the work force, the fact is, women's wages continue to lag far\nbehind men's. The economic position of women on the whole has deteri-\norated, despite affirmative action and equal pay laws and-the movement\nof some women into a few traditionally male-dominated occupations.\n\nAccording to a 2003 report from the General Accounting Office on Women's\nEarnings, which examined 38 years of data, found a 20 percent earnings\ngap between women and men that could not be explained, even when\naccounting for demographic and work-related factors such as occupation,\nindustry, race, marital status and job tenure.\n\nThis legislation would ensure equal pay for individuals within a\nprotected class, as defined in the Executive Law, who are conducting\nsubstantially similar work.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
